Why: Linux hotkey fires a chord addressed to a window through the menu item that carries it as its shortcut (AT-SPI key binding, then the item's action under the focus guard), so Ctrl+S saves in the background without the uinput virtual keyboard that Xvfb and most sessions cannot create; a chord whose item is disabled is refused with that reason instead of a keyboard-route error.

